# Personal Portfolio Website
### HTML5 Semantic Structure & Accessibility

## 📌 Project Overview

This project is a multi-page personal portfolio website developed using modern HTML5 and CSS3.

The main objective is to build a well-structured, accessible, and SEO-friendly website by following semantic HTML5 standards and WCAG accessibility guidelines.

The website introduces personal information, technical skills, projects, and contact details.

## 🎯 Project Objectives

- Understand modern HTML5 semantic elements.
- Create a multi-page personal portfolio website.
- Implement accessibility features using ARIA attributes.
- Develop an accessible, keyboard-navigable contact form.
- Apply SEO-friendly meta tags.
- Improve website usability and responsiveness.
- Test website quality using Google Lighthouse.

## 🛠️ Technologies Used

- HTML5
- CSS3
- Visual Studio Code
- Google Chrome
- Google Lighthouse

## 📂 Project Structure

```text
personal-portfolio/
│
├── index.html
├── about.html
├── projects.html
├── contact.html
├── style.css
└── README.md
```

## 🌟 Key Features

### 1. Semantic HTML5 Structure
Uses meaningful HTML5 elements:

- `<header>` – Website header.
- `<nav>` – Navigation menu.
- `<main>` – Main page content.
- `<section>` – Groups related content.
- `<article>` – Independent project content.
- `<footer>` – Website footer.

### 2. Accessibility Features

- ARIA labels for navigation.
- Proper labels for form fields.
- Keyboard-friendly navigation.
- Visible keyboard focus indicators.
- Meaningful heading structure.
- Accessible form controls.

### 3. SEO Optimization

- Meaningful page titles.
- Meta descriptions.
- UTF-8 character encoding.
- Responsive viewport configuration.
- Descriptive navigation links.

### 4. Responsive Design

The website uses CSS media queries to improve usability on desktop computers, tablets, and mobile devices.

### 5. Contact Form

The contact page includes:
- Name field.
- Email field.
- Message field.
- Required field validation.
- Submit button.

**Note:** The contact form is a front-end demonstration. A backend or form service is required to actually deliver messages.

## 📄 Website Pages

| Page | Description |
|---|---|
| Home | Introduction and welcome message |
| About | Personal information and skills |
| Projects | Programming projects |
| Contact | Contact form |

## 🚀 How to Run the Project

1. Download or clone the project.
2. Open the project folder in Visual Studio Code.
3. Open `index.html`.
4. Right-click and select Open with Live Server.
5. The portfolio website will open in your browser.
6. Navigate through the Home, About, Projects, and Contact pages.

## 🧪 Testing and Validation

The website can be tested using Google Chrome Lighthouse.

Testing categories:

- Accessibility
- SEO
- Best Practices
- Performance

**Expected Goal:** Achieve 100/100 in Lighthouse Accessibility and SEO audits.

Note: These are target scores, not guaranteed results. Manual accessibility testing is also required to assess WCAG compliance.

## ♿ Accessibility Standards

This project aims to follow WCAG 2.2 Level AA principles:

- Perceivable
- Operable
- Understandable
- Robust

## 📈 Learning Outcomes

After completing this project, I learned:

- Basics of HTML5 and CSS3.
- Semantic website structure.
- Accessibility and ARIA attributes.
- Creating accessible HTML forms.
- SEO optimization techniques.
- Responsive website design.
- Testing websites using Lighthouse.

